Subject: vm: suspend a blocked coroutine instead of parking its thread

The coroutine that runs a call into Wasm can yield `Blocked(future)`
when a host wait would park the thread; `on_wasm_stack` becomes a loop
that polls the future on the OS thread's stack and resumes the coroutine
when it completes, propagating the wait to an enclosing coroutine if
there is one so that no OS thread stack holds guest state. A guest
thread that waits therefore has its whole state in memory, which is what
freezing and forking a process rely on.

Every thread-local access on the resume path goes through a function
that is never inlined: the compiler materialises a thread-local's
address once per function, and a coroutine resumed on another thread
would otherwise write into the old thread's block.

Nothing changes until `suspend::enable` is called: the loop runs exactly
once per call, as before. Traps still unwind through the yielder.

